---
import Section from './Section.astro'
---

<Section id="about">
  <h2 class="text-text-primary mb-8 text-2xl font-semibold" id="about-heading">About</h2>
  <div class="text-text-secondary flex max-w-2xl flex-col gap-8 text-lg leading-relaxed">
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
      <p class="text-text-primary font-medium">This is how it happened.</p>
      <p>
        It was video day in school. Everybody seated. We had that classic rolling TV cart with the
        VCR and a VHS tape playing... and out of nowhere:
      </p>
      <p>Boom! The TV went pitch black.</p>
      <p>
        The teacher had no clue what was going on, so she hit the red power button again and
        everything came back on. A minute later, it shut off again, this time the VCR. By the third
        time the TV crashed, that was the end of the show: no more educational video.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>'Damn piece of junk'</em>, she muttered under her breath in pure frustration... or so
        she thought, because the whole class heard it.
      </p>
      <p>And yet, nobody in class had seen me. But my friend Frank...</p>
      <p>...he instantly knew it was me.</p>
      <p>
        He knew me well and <span class="text-text-primary font-medium"
          >I was 16 and had a jailbroken Symbian Nokia 3650</span
        > loaded with a universal remote app.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
      <p>
        It was 2003 and however stupid my videos were at the time, I was always short of space, so I
        was doing market research surveys for small amounts of money. Every cent, every euro, going
        toward storage for that little machine of joy.
      </p>
      <p>Within days, I had turned my phone into a jukebox. Full mp3 playback.</p>
      <p>
        No one around me had that. A few people in my neighborhood had phones by then, but I was the
        only one with a hacked operating system running music on hardware that wasn't built for it.
      </p>
      <p>So people would see it and ask: <em>"How did you do that?"</em></p>
      <p class="text-text-primary font-medium">
        But I didn't have a clean answer. Just saw something I wanted, found the path, and kept
        going until it worked.
      </p>
      <p>
        Same thing when I was dismantling old PCs my uncle passed along before throwing them out —
        taking the working parts, figuring out what fit with what, building machines from the
        scraps.
      </p>
      <p>
        Or learning English not from a class (I hated it), but from tech forums, because that was
        the only way to get to the information I needed.
      </p>
      <p>Of course we all learn fast when we have a reason.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
      <p>
        I loved that stuff. I spent hours locked in my room, constantly glued to that box filled
        with text, one hour after another until I got what I wanted. Installing an OS, pranking a
        friend, bypassing restrictions...
      </p>
      <p>
        So much so that I ended up becoming the family's go-to tech guy. Whenever someone didn't
        know how to use something on their phone, they called me.
      </p>
      <p>
        I once went phone shopping with my mother (she asked) and told the saleswoman the phone had
        to have <span class="text-text-primary font-medium">WAP 2.0</span>,—1.0 wasn't gonna cut
        it—and at least <span class="text-text-primary font-medium">65,536 colors</span>, because
        4,096 was basically pre-historic.
      </p>
      <span class="text-text-primary font-medium"
        >The clerk looked at me like I was possessed or speaking alien.</span
      >
      <p>
        And honestly, it even crossed my mind that I could do her job 10 times better than she
        could. But of course, I was into the technology itself; retail, not so much.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
      <p>Back then, naturally, people kept asking me: <em>'What are you gonna study?'</em></p>
      <p>
        And ever since I was a little kid, working in tech had always been my dream. But because I
        couldn't see the path forward, I thought it'd be impossible for me to ever get there.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because I'd fallen into the same bullshit trap as so many others: believing that to be a
        programmer, you have to be some kind of genius or a total math whiz.
      </p>
      <p>Yes, people born with a kind of brain I thought I didn't have.</p>
      <p>Turns out...</p>
      <p class="text-text-primary font-medium">
        By all observable evidence, I was already doing what programmers do.
      </p>
      <p>Still, I didn't see it that way, at all.</p>
      <p>
        It only took a bad experience with a math teacher in secondary school to convince me I
        wasn't that kind of person and I didn't have this kind of brain. So I filed it under "not
        for me" and moved on.
      </p>
      <p>That belief held for a long time.</p>
      <p>And I ended up in hospitality. Not by accident — I was good at it. Eleven years.</p>
      <p>
        Bar operations at Grupo Dani García, Platea Madrid — Michelin-star level, where there's no
        version of "close enough." Every service, every day, at the highest standard without
        exception.
      </p>
      <p>And I loved that part cause it felt familiar.</p>
      <p>
        The same obsessive pattern: understand the system, find where it breaks, fix it before it
        breaks.
      </p>
      <p>Different domain. Same instinct.</p>
      <p>
        Alongside it, I built a network of affiliate and SEO sites that actually made money. Not
        hobby projects — systems I researched, structured, and ran like a business. I had to learn
        search mechanics, conversion logic, content strategy. From scratch.
      </p>
      <p>Because I wanted to, and because I had discovered a fun problem I wanted to solve.</p>
      <p>
        Then DeFi, because finance became the next hard thing. Three years reading whitepapers, not
        summaries. Starting with Bitcoin, understanding the mechanics, not just the headlines.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
      <p>But at some point the story changed...</p>
      <p>...and I came across David Deutsch's argument:</p>
      <p><strong>All problems are soluble given the right knowledge.</strong></p>
      <p>And it's an uncomfortable idea because it removes excuses.</p>
      <p>
        If a problem persists unsolved, it's a knowledge deficit — find the knowledge, solve the
        problem.
      </p>
      <p>So I ran that against the belief I'd been carrying since I can remember:</p>
      <p><em>I'm not made for programming.</em></p>
      <p>And I'm happy I did, because it didn't survive the contact with the principle.</p>
      <p>
        Because the kid who modded phone operating systems at sixteen, who built PCs from salvage,
        who learned English from documentation because he had no other path — that kid had been
        doing this the entire time.
      </p>
      <p>The only thing missing was the belief that he <em>could</em>...</p>
      <p>...and that was just a knowledge problem too.</p>
      <p>So I finally learned to code.</p>
      <p>
        And I did it the same way I'd learned everything else: <span
          class="text-text-primary font-medium"
          >identify the problem, find the knowledge, go until it works.</span
        >
      </p>
      <p>
        DeFiScout exists because I read the Uniswap V3 whitepaper before touching the SDK. That's
        not a flex — it's just the only way I know how to work.
      </p>
      <p>
        SipMatch exists because I had eleven years of bar expertise and wanted to see what a
        recommendation engine built from actual domain knowledge looked like, not from a tutorial.
      </p>
      <p>
        And <del>despite</del> thanks to all those mistakes, <a
          href="mailto:hello@dalon.so"
          class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:opacity-80">here I am</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>
